  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 326 AM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nSurface and upper-air observations from 00Z show subtle changes to \nthe overall synoptic pattern over the last 12-24 hours. Over New \nEngland, the upper trough has caught up to the closed low/Nor'Easter \nembedded within the broader upper-level ridge. This system will \ngradually be reabsorbed and carried northeast back to the Atlantic \nover the coming days. Meanwhile, the upper trough across the western \nCONUS is set to continue amplifying. As it does so, the base of the \ntrough is expected extend flush against the southern U.S. border, \nclosing itself off from the ~130 kt jet streak protruding into \nBritish Columbia. Between now and Tuesday, conditions look rather \nquiet as the axis of the ridge leading the western trough builds \ninto the region. Some patchy fog may be able to develop across \nnorthern Missouri during the early morning hours on Monday where \nwinds are light and cloud cover is minimized. Warmer conditions are \nexpected across the region both Monday and Tuesday, with high \ntemperatures in the mid/upper 70s along and north of the US-36 \ncorridor. Further south, maximum temperatures are more likely to \nsettle in the upper 70s and low 80s. \n\nThe dry weather will not last long, however, with rain chances \nreturning as soon as late Tuesday evening. Latest guidance suggests \nthat the remnants of weakening Hurricane Polo are to lift toward the \nsouthern New Mexico/Texas border on Tuesday, by which point residual \ntropical moisture and vorticity would be ingested into the \naforementioned cyclone. This system is then progged to surge \nnortheastward into portions of the Southern and Central Plains, \nintroducing heavy rainfall and flooding concerns. Tuesday night into \nearly Wednesday, showers and thunderstorms are expected to enshroud \nmuch of the Plains as the low level jet intensifies in the vicinity \nof the extremely moist airmass. Showers and storms will be most \nlikely across the northwestern and western portions of the forecast \nTuesday night, gradually expanding in coverage throughout the day on \nWednesday. As things stand now, all portions of the forecast area \nhave >80% PoPs sometime between Tuesday and Thursday night; for \nmost, the increased likelihood of rainfall arrives late Wednesday \nnight into Thursday when PWATs between 1.8 and 2.1 nose into the \narea. For context, these values sit roughly around the 99th \npercentile compared to both the NAEFS and GEFS climatologies. \n\nWhile confidence in rain is high, confidence in total rainfall \naccumulations is certainly less so. Looking back at LREF \nprobabilities from the middle of last week up through the most \nrecent run, there is a clear downward trend in total expected QPF. \nHowever, this alone fails to tell the whole story; instead, high \nuncertainty is confirmed by ensemble spreads. For total rainfall \naccumulations by Friday evening, there is a 1.5 inch spread between \nthe 25th and 75th percentiles across the forecast area. For the 10th \nand 90th percentiles, spread spikes to about 3.5 inches. Needless to \nsay, there are substantial challenges in skillfully assigning total \naccumulations at this time. This is compounded by unclear frontal \nboundary placements which are pivotal in determining key areas to \nhone in on for flooding potential. Despite the level of uncertainty \nremaining, widespread accumulations of 1.5\" to 2\" seem to be the \nmost likely solution at this junction. That said, it will be key to \nfollow system evolution and model trends over the coming days as \nthis will guide future updates.",
